gm-method debugging lessons from a 'terrain surface not visible + veg/models not occluded + low fps' multi-cause hunt on a WebGL planet-backdrop + three.ez InstancedMesh consumer: (1) WITNESS RELIABILITY: on a preserveDrawingBuffer:false canvas, BOTH gl.readPixels-from-outside-the-rAF AND ctx.drawImage(canvas) return [0,0,0,0] (lie). The reliable readback is gl.readPixels called INSIDE the render loop right after the draw you want (wrap the draw fn via a window-exposed global), OR a real page.screenshot() (compositor output, truthful) which you then Read as an image. A 15KB png == near-flat; 500KB+ == real content - but only trust it after render settles (~20-26s for this heavy boot). (2) 'invisible terrain' was NOT a draw failure - the planet drew with afterErr:0 but blew out to pure white [255,255,255]: a backdrop's atmosphere/aerial-perspective composite was disabled. A consumer flag forcing the backdrop's half-res-water/effects FBO OFF (chasing a depth-share fix) also killed the atmosphere pass -> white-out. Lesson: a backdrop's 'water'/'half-res' FBO pass often ALSO carries atmosphere/tone; disabling it for depth reasons can white-out the surface. (3) DEPTH-SHARE: a backdrop that renders into its own depth-FBO and upscales COLOR-only gives the consumer scene no depth to test -> consumer geometry draws OVER the backdrop. Fix = blitFramebuffer(backdropFBO depth -> default framebuffer, DEPTH_BUFFER_BIT, full-res 1:1) after the composite, gated by an opt-in flag, WITH the consumer rendering autoClear=false so the blitted depth survives. (4) three.ez/instanced-mesh 'instanceIndex undeclared identifier' on three r183: the lib appends its `attribute uint instanceIndex` decl onto three's <batching_pars_vertex> chunk, but a custom/depth material's vertex shader may not include that chunk -> decl missing while the lib's injected uniforms block uses it. Fix in the lib's _onBeforeCompile: prepend the decl under USE_INSTANCING_INDIRECT when <batching_pars_vertex> is ABSENT (guard on the #include, not on a regex of the unexpanded source, else you double-declare = redefinition on chunk-present materials). Capture the FULL failing vertex shader source via an addInitScript getContext->compileShader hook to see what chunks actually expanded. (5) SHADER ERROR FLOOD AS FPS KILLER: a per-frame 'useProgram: program not valid' from one broken instanced material tanks fps far more than the missing draw - eliminate by ?veg=none / hiding the mesh to confirm it's the cause. (6) BRIDGE PATTERN: when a sibling-SDK or upstream-lib fix isn't published yet, an idempotent postinstall patch script (skip-if-marker-present) keeps the consumer correct + deployable until upstream ships it; a sibling repo's fix also goes in its source for its own publish cycle.